   438	    /// @dev Deployed by the factory, so `Distribution.factory` is this contract on every clone.
   439	    address public immutable implementation = address(new Distribution{salt: bytes32(0)}());
   440	
   441	    /// @notice Deploy and initialise a clone at `predict(salt)`.
   442	    /// @param salt Its top 20 bytes must equal `msg.sender`. `msg.value` rides the CREATE2, so a
   443	    /// deposit lands in the clone without a `Received` log.
   444	    /// @dev Three properties the dapp has to know about this address:
   445	    /// 1. It depends on this factory and `salt` only — not on the owner, delay, description or table.
   446	    ///    So the sender fixes the address; the owner does not enter it. One EOA calls `create` with
   447	    ///    the same salt on every chain and passes the vault as `owner_`, and the clone is owned by
   448	    ///    that vault from its first block at one address, with no ownership transfer ever needed.
   449	    ///    Each chain is a separate contract: transferring ownership on one changes neither the salt
   450	    ///    nor any other chain, so the deploying key never needs to own anything.
   451	    /// 2. A second `create` with a used salt burns the whole gas limit before `DeploymentFailed`: a
   452	    ///    CREATE2 collision consumes every unit forwarded to it. Read `predict(salt)` with
   453	    ///    `eth_getCode` first and refuse a non-empty answer.
   454	    /// 3. Same-address-everywhere is this factory's address, not a clone's claim on its own. The
   455	    ///    factory is nonce-addressed where it is deployed; a byte-identical deploy through a
   456	    ///    deterministic deployer is a later milestone's one-way door.
   457	    function create(
   458	        address owner_,
   459	        uint32 delay_,
   460	        string calldata description_,
   461	        Distribution.Beneficiary[] calldata table_,
   462	        bytes32 salt
   463	    ) external payable returns (address distribution) {
   464	        address impl = implementation;
   465	        uint256 prefix = uint256(salt) >> 96;
